#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 840.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116360} :activity/id #uuid "ac88fe79-2634-585b-ad64-78810162af15" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049291 :topic/id #uuid "ea2d0660-7338-5607-a4c7-0bf354713fdf" :topic/math-academy-id 1741} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116360 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116437 :tutorial/content "To find the limit of a vector function, we compute the limit of each component individually. So, if $\\mathbf f (t) = x(t) \\ \\mathbf {i} + y(t) \\ \\mathbf {j} +z(t) \\ \\mathbf {k}$ is our vector function, then the limit is computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{t\\to a} \\mathbf f (t) = \\bigg[ \\lim_{t \\to a} x(t) \\bigg] \\mathbf {i} + \\bigg[ \\lim_{t \\to a}y(t) \\bigg] \\mathbf {j} +\\bigg[ \\lim_{t \\to a}z(t) \\bigg] \\mathbf {k}\n$$\n\nThe limit on the left exists *if and only if* all the limits on the right exist too.\n\nFor example, suppose we are given the vector function\n\n$$\n\\mathbf f (t) = \\left( 5+t \\right) \\ \\mathbf {i} +  (t^{2}-1) \\ \\mathbf {j} + \\sin{t} \\ \\mathbf {k},\n$$\n\nand we want to compute the limit $\\lim\\limits_{t \\to 0} \\mathbf f (t).$ To do this, we find the limit of each component function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t) & =[\\,{{{lim}_{t\\to 0}(5+t)}_{⏟}}_{=\\,5}\\,]i+[\\,{{{lim}_{t\\to 0}({t}^{2}-1)\\,}_{⏟}}_{=\\,-1}]j+[\\,{{{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,}_{⏟}}_{=\\,0}]k \\\\  & =5\\text{ }i+(-1)\\text{ }j+0\\text{ }k \\\\  & =⟨5,\\,-1,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "26262318-035c-588b-8625-fb16e1aa3f7b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2104 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "bfded506-1b64-52cf-a9f3-10031e2d9fd8" :step/math-academy-id 8068 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116362}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116361 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116438 :tutorial/content "Many properties of limits of scalar functions extend naturally to limits of vector functions. For instance, if $\\mathbf f(t)$ and $\\mathbf g(t)$ are vector functions and $p,q$ are constants, then\n\n$$\n\\lim_{t \\to a}\\left[p \\ \\mathbf f(t) + q \\ \\mathbf g(t)\\right] =  p \\ \\lim_{t \\to a}\\mathbf f(t) + q \\ \\lim_{t \\to a}\\mathbf g(t).\n$$\n\nWe also have the following property for the magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to a}‖f(t)‖ & =‖{lim}_{t\\to a}f(t)‖,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\left\\| \\mathbf f(t) \\right\\|$ denotes the magnitude of the vector $\\mathbf f(t)$." :tutorial/id #uuid "59b68b30-750a-5143-9b2c-090e7a8d7075" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2105 :tutorial/title "Basic Properties of Limits of Vector Functions"} :step/id #uuid "41b754d8-e7b1-57e5-927f-834d11a20f5c" :step/math-academy-id 8069 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116364}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116362 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115386 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114966 :question/id #uuid "4e4541ff-5c48-5300-ac29-e48111fee506" :question/math-academy-id "e-2394"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ac33006-2d6d-5ee2-9749-a1f3dea17869" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048148} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049186} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049769} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049927} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051036}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115754} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115765} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367247} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367261} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367992} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390841} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563661} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563667} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563714} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563738} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563754}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Limit of a Vector Function"} :step/id #uuid "0344b29b-2e6e-541e-846b-ffd10663466d" :step/math-academy-id 8180 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116361}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116363 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116439 :tutorial/content "For two vector functions, the limit of a product is equal to the product of the limits:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to a}[f(t)\\cdot g(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to a}f(t)\\cdot {lim}_{t\\to a}g(t) \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to a}[f(t)\\times g(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to a}f(t)\\times {lim}_{t\\to a}g(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $\\mathbf f(t)\\cdot \\mathbf g(t)$ and $\\mathbf f(t) \\times \\mathbf g(t)$ are the dot product and the cross product of $\\mathbf f(t)$ and $\\mathbf g(t),$ respectively." :tutorial/id #uuid "69d5def3-e31a-5450-9cf7-698d5ddcfda0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2109 :tutorial/title "Limits of Vector Products"} :step/id #uuid "1c55acc4-6497-53a3-9f34-51c7788b4e52" :step/math-academy-id 8182 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116366}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116364 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115387 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114967 :question/id #uuid "b5f3f5c9-3d28-5fee-8d67-d49d743ae474" :question/math-academy-id "e-2395"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "610e605b-30f6-574b-b149-3717cfd4d72a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051021}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115755} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115766} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367268} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367275} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367282} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367985} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368730} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563662} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563693} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563712} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563716} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563752}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Limit of the Magnitude of a Vector Function"} :step/id #uuid "31b44d63-7b11-5092-9165-cc0cea2496e1" :step/math-academy-id 8183 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116365}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116365 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115388 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114968 :question/id #uuid "c2055711-815d-5385-8f31-19cc81f20698" :question/math-academy-id "e-2396"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "